## a b s t r a c t
Article history:  Received 10 July 2019  Revised 9 August 2019  Accepted 12 August 2019  Available online 12 August 2019
Keywords:  Hybrid supercapacitor  Metal organic framework  MOF  ZIF-67  Metal oxide  Nanoporous  Hollow spheres  \(\mathrm{CuCo_{2}O_{4}}\)
Design of complex hollow nanostructures of two or more transition metal oxides seems to be necessary to answer the demand for novel energy storage electrodes owning outstanding performance for advanced developments of modern electronics. Herein, we develop a metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) assistance self-templated method for the synthesis of double-shell \(\mathrm{CuCo_{2}O_{4}}\) hollow spheres as battery-type electrode material with a large surface area of \(93\;\mathrm{m}^{2}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\). This electrode material reveals excellent electrochemical performance with an ultrahigh specific capacity of \(701\;\mathrm{C}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) at \(2\;\mathrm{A}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\). Additionally, remarkably high cycling performance is exhibited with maintaining more than \(93.6\%\) of the initial capacity after 6000 cycles. The assembly of the double-shell hollow spheres electrode with reduced graphene oxide (rGO) electrode in an asymmetric cell results in a high-performance supercapacitor with an energy density of \(38.4\;\mathrm{Wh}\;\mathrm{kg}^{-1}\) and a power density of \(16\;\mathrm{kW}\;\mathrm{kg}^{-1}\), that is remarkably higher than that of conventional supercapacitors and comparable with Ni-MH batteries. Additionally, we display that assembling two asymmetric devices in series could effectively power blue, green, and red LED indicators. The excellent electrochemical performance of the ZCCO electrode shows its high potential for the production of advanced energy storage devices.
