## 1. Introduction
Electrochemical energy storage and conversion systems including batteries, fuel cells, and supercapacitors are under development aimed to replacing fossil fuels [1,2]. Among different
electrochemical energy storage devices, supercapacitors have been highlighted and attracted much attention as advanced energy storage devices [3–5]. However, they suffer from relatively low energy density (normally lower than \(10\,\mathsf{W h}\,\mathsf{k g}^{-1}\)). To overcome this shortcoming, nowadays researchers are trying to make a device to store electrical energy such that it has the advantages of battery and supercapacitor together and simultaneously not including their disadvantages. Such a device is known as hybrid supercapacitors (HSCs) or supercapattery [6,7]. HSCs include a pseudocapacitive or battery-type electrode along with electric double-layer capacitive electrode as a power source acting as a Faradaic redox reaction and electrostatic interaction, respectively [8]. Nevertheless, the whole-scale application of such devices are still limited due to the low rate capability and cyclic life of electroactive materials [9]. Consequently, more researches are essential for electroactive materials with enhanced electrochemical performance. According to the inherent interactions and synergetic effects, novel nanostructures with different transition metal components reveal better performance in HSC systems [10]. Binary metal oxides deliver higher electrical conductivity and are more rich in redox reactions, which results in an improvement in electrochemical efficiency [11,12]. The excessive electrochemical activity of cobalt promotes the application of this metal in HSC electrode materials; however, it suffers from low conductivity [13]. Our previous studies revealed that the bimetallic Cu-Co material shows excellent activity and rate performance in HSCs [14].
In addition to the smart selection of electrode material, the performance of supercapacitors significantly depends on the surface morphology [15]. Enhancement in conductivity and surface area could be attained by means of novel nano-sized structures with micro/nano ranged pores, which improves the mass and charge transport in material structure due to the higher weight fraction of active species [16,17]. Accordingly, hollow nanostructures with pores and flakes are known as promising candidates for this purpose [18,19]. Complex hollow structures including two or more shells offer higher energy density and can lead to dominating the low packaging density of single-shell hollow structures due to the emptying inside spacing. The complex hollow structure of various metal oxides has been synthesized using different soft or hard-template and self-templating approaches [20,21]. The self-templating procedures without hard or soft templates are more favorable owning to eliminate heterogeneous growth, simple synthesis method, lower synthesis costs, easier scale-up and better control of size and uniformity [22]. In this regards, significant studies focused on these structures to extend advanced electrodes with high efficiency by self-templating methods. For example, onionlike hollow spheres of copper-cobalt spinel was synthesized by means of thermal decomposition of internal solid spheres [23]. However, it seems to be still challenging to synthesize well-defined hollow structure with sufficient active sites using presented conventional methods.
Recently, some strategies include the application of metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) and polyol-based metal alkoxides providing advanced opportunities for the production of complex nanostructured materials [24–26]. MOFs are known as materials with high porosity according to the low density, periodic nature and extremely high specific surface area [27]. Recent investigations showed that due to the sacrificial nature of templates and metal-based precursors of these structures, MOF networks could be considered as bifunctional materials for the construction of hollow structures [28,29]. For instance, the porous hollow structure of \(\mathsf{C o}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}\) was synthesized by Huang et al. using the calcination of ZIF-67 MOF as precursor under static air. The obtained structure revealed a reasonable specific capacitance of about \(1100\,\mathrm{F}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) at a constant current density equal to \(1.25\,\mathsf{A}\,\mathsf{g}^{-1}\) [30]. More recently, Wang et al. designed and synthesized nanocage of NiCo-LDH/CoS2 structure using metal-organic frameworks on nanotubes of \(\mathsf{M n O}_{2}\) that revealed an enhanced surface area with good conductivity [31]. Furthermore, Lou et al. fabricated an anode of LIBs using nanosized \(\mathsf{C o S}_{2}\) bubble-like structures via the thermal decomposition of ZIF-67 MOF with hollow structure. The attained system showed high rate capacity along with good cyclic stability [32]. Kuang et al. have synthesized a Cu-Co bimetal embedded \(N\)-doped carbon framework structure via the thermal pyrolysis of ZIF-67 formed on nanowires of copper hydroxide and applied for redox reactions of hydrogen production [33]. Hence, the delicate design of MOFs with multi-metal centers could be used to construct multi-component metal oxide with designable structures and compositions.
