Accordingly, we design and develop nanoporous double-shelled \(\mathsf{C u C o}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4}\) hollow sphere using a simple MOF based self-templating procedure for high-performance HSCs. As shown in scheme 1, in the first step, cobalt copper glycerol (CC-gly) spherical precursors were synthesis and selected as the metal ion source. Afterward, uniform bimetallic copper-cobalt zeolitic imidazolate framework, as a subclass of MOFs, nanocubes are formed around the CC-gly sphere core (ZCC-gly). Lastly, the synthesized ZCC-gly yolk-shell sphere is converted to a double-shell hollow sphere of \(\mathsf{C u C o}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4}\) (ZCCO) via an annealing treatment under air atmosphere. According to the desired composition and noticeable structural advantages, the as-prepared electrode exhibited enhanced electrochemical performance in a three-electrode system. Additionally, the fabricated HSC based on the ZCCO and reduced graphene oxide (rGO) as the positive and negative electrodes respectively, revealed significant energy and power density along with excellent cycle life. The practical application of the ZCCO//rGO devices as a power source for advanced electronics was checked by powering several light-emitting diodes (LEDs).
## 2. Experimental
## 2.1. Preparation of copper-cobalt glycerate@copper-cobalt ZIF (ZCC-gly)
In order to synthesis ZCC-gly, \(1\,\mathrm{g}\) of 2-methylimidazole was dissolved in \(14\,\mathrm{mL}\) of deionized water as solution A. Afterwards \(30\,\mathrm{mg}\) of the above CC-gly precursor (supporting information) was redispersed into \(2\,\mathrm{mL}\) of ethanol as the solution B. Then, solution B was quickly injected into solution A and subsequently stirred for \(30\,\mathrm{min}\). The attained purple suspension (ZCC-gly) was then centrifuged, washed several times with absolute ethanol and dried at \(60\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(24\,\mathrm{h}\).
## 2.2. Preparation of cobalt copper oxide (ZCCO) double-shell hollow sphere
2.2. Preparation of cobalt copper oxide (ZCCO) double-shell hollow sphereTo attain nanoporous double-shell \(\mathsf{C u C o}_{2}0_{4}\) hollow microspheres (ZCCO), the as-synthesized ZCC-gly powder was calcinated at \(350\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(2\,\mathrm{h}\) under static air with a heating ramp of \(1\ {}^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\,\mathrm{min}^{-1}\) (Scheme 1). In order to investigate the effect of bimetallic CuCo-ZIF on the structural properties, electrochemical and superconducting behavior of synthesized samples; the bulk sample without the addition of CuCo-ZIF (donated as CCO) was synthesized and investigated as reported previously [14].
## 2.3. Electrochemical measurements
2.3. Electrochemical measurementsThe electrochemical properties of the synthesized samples were studied by means of constant current voltammetry and galvanostatic charge-discharge aperture. The as-prepared electrodes were tested in a three-electrode system and two-electrode cell. Typically, three electrodes including work, auxiliary, and reference electrodes are prepared and embedded in a \(3\,\mathrm{M}\) aqueous solution of potassium hydroxide. The working electrode was fabricated using a slurry containing \(80\%\) of the as-synthesized active material, \(10\%\) carbon black (to improve electrical conductivity) and \(10\%\) PVDF (as the adhesive for attaching the active material to electrode surface). The coating of the electrode was performed by means of spraying the attained slurry on a Ni foam and drying in the oven. The mass loading on each electrode was about \(2\,\mathrm{mg\,cm}^{-2}\).In order to calculate the specific capacity (C) through the charge-discharge diagrams, the following equation is applied where \(I\) is the discharge current in terms of A, \(\varDelta t\) is the discharging time in seconds and \(m\) is the total mass of the active substance [34]:
In order to calculate the specific capacity (C) through the charge-discharge diagrams, the following equation is applied where I is the discharge current in terms of A, \(\varDelta t\) is the discharging time in seconds and \(m\) is the total mass of the active substance [34]:
\[
C=\frac{I\times\Delta t}{m}(\mathbf{C}.\mathbf{g}^{-1})
\]
The asymmetric supercapacitor (two-electrode cell) was assembled based on the ZCCO as the positive electrode and rGO as a negative electrode, separated using a cellulosic paper. For this equality, it is necessary to determine the mass ratio indicated on the positive and negative electrodes based on the charge balance theory [35]:
\[
Q=C\times m\times\Delta E
\]
\[Q=C\times m\times\Delta E\]Furthermore, the mass ratio could be calculated using the following equation [30]:
\[
\frac{m^{+}}{m^{-}}=\frac{C^{-}\times\Delta E^{-}}{C^{+}\times\Delta E^{+}}
\]
\[m^{+}=\frac{C^{-}\times\Delta E^{-}}{C^{+}\times\Delta E^{+}}\]Based on this equation, the mass ratio between ZCCO and rGO electrodes was calculated to be about 1.3.5 satisfying the chargebalance theory. Thus, 2 and \(7\,\mathrm{mg}\) of ZCCO and rGO was applied for each electrode with a geometric surface of \(1\;\mathrm{cm}^{2}\), respectively.The power and specific energy of a supercapacitor are known as important determining parameters for a supercapacitor. The amount of specific energy (E) and the power (P) of a supercapacitor can be calculated according to Eqs. (4) and (5) [7,36]:
The power and specific energy of a supercapacitor are known as important determining parameters for a supercapacitor. The amount of specific energy (E) and the power (P) of a supercapacitor can be calculated according to Eqs. (4) and (5) [7,36]:
\[
E=\frac{1}{3.6}\int_{0}^{V_{max}}Vdt
\]
\[
P=\frac{E}{\Delta t}
\]
\[P=\frac{E}{\Delta t}\]where the unit of specific energy is \(\mathsf{W h\,k g}^{-1}\) and the unit of power is \(\mathsf{W\,k g}^{-1}\).
## 2.4. Characterization
Different characterization techniques were applied to investigate and evaluate the properties of the synthesized samples. The X-ray powder diffraction (XRD) patterns were collected using a Bruker (D8 Advance, Germany) instrument using CuKα radiation as the X-ray source and \(2\theta=5{-}80^{\circ}\) with the step size of \(0.05^{\circ}/s\). the X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) analysis was conducted using a VG ESCALAB MKII spectrometer with \(\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}\ \mathbf{K}_{\alpha}\) X-ray source. Field-emission scanning electron microscopy (FESEM) was applied in order to investigate the morphology of the samples using a MIRA3 TESCAN-XMU device. Additionally, transmission electron microscopic (TEM) images were recorded via a \(100\,\mathrm{kV}\) Philips EM 208S electron microscope in order to study the structural properties of the samples. The \(\Nu_{2}\) adsorption-desorption isotherms were determined at \(77\,\mathrm{K}\) using an ASAP-2010 surface area analyzer.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Characterization
3. Results and discussion3.1. CharacterizationThe XRD patterns of the as-synthesized samples are demonstrated in Fig. 1a and b to investigate their crystallinity and phase purity. The XRD patterns of CC-gly and ZCC-gly precursors (Fig. 1a) show the amorphous nature of the CC-gly sample. On the other hand, once the bimetallic CuCo-ZIF is formed on the surface of CC-gly precursor, the attained structure revealed its crystalline nature. In fact, by the transformation of the solid precursor in the presence of 2-methylimidazole, ZIF-67 is formed on the surface and all the peaks of the pattern refer to the presence of ZIF-67 phase without evident peak for residues. It confirms the formation of ZIF-67 crystals on the surface of CuCo-gly precursor [37,38]. The XRD patterns of CCO and ZCCO are also demonstrated in Fig. 1b. As shown, the most peaks of both samples are in good agreement with the standard patterns related to the cubic crystals of copper-cobalt spinel (JCPDS File No. 001-1155). The diffraction peaks at \(2\theta=19.1\), 31.4, 37.0, 39.0, 45.1, 56.1, 59.6 and \(65.7^{\circ}\) can be perfectly indexed to (1 1 1), (2 2 0), (3 1 1), (2 2 2), (4 0 0), (4 2 2), (5 1 1) and (4 4 0) crystal plane reflections of \(\mathsf{C u C o}_{2}0_{4}\) respectively [39,40]. No significant peaks were assigned to other oxide forms such as CuO, CoO or \(\mathsf{C o O}_{2}\) indicating the high purity of the synthesized \(\mathsf{C u C o}_{2}0_{4}\) hollow spheres.The BET and BJH methods could be applied to examine the porous structure, specific surface area, and pore size distribution of samples that are important parameters affecting the performance of electrodes (Fig. 1c) [41]. The BET specific surface area of the ZCCO sample is \(93\;\mathrm{m}^{2}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) indicating that the ZCCO sample has a reasonable surface area and high porosity, which could play a key role in facilitating the accessibility of the ion-transport pathways [40]. In addition, it can provide more electroactive sites for electrochemical reactions in energy storage and conversion applications [42].The XPS analysis was implemented to further characterize the near-surface and elemental composition of the ZCCO sample. The high-resolution X-ray photoelectron spectra of Cu 2p and Co 2p of the double-shelled ZCCO hollow spheres are indicated in Fig. 1d. The Co \(2{\tt p}_{3/2}\) and Co \(2{\tt p}_{1/2}\) peaks considered at 779.9 and \(794.8\,\mathrm{eV}\) with a splitting of about \(15\,\mathrm{eV}\) and two shake-up satellites prove the co-existence of \({\mathsf{C o}}^{3+}\) and \({\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}\) (as a mixed matrix) [43]. The Cu 2p spectrum is best fitted with two spin-orbit doublets at \(934.2\,\mathrm{eV}\) and \(954.2\,\mathrm{eV}\) corresponding to Cu \(2{\tt p}_{3/2}\) and Cu \(2{\tt p}_{1/2}\), which are characteristic of \({\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}\) [44,45]. The characteristic satellite peaks at higher-bond of binding energies are related to \(\mathsf{C u}(\Pi)\) oxidation state. The peaks at 529.8 and \(31.3\,\mathrm{eV}\) of O 1s spectrum,
stand for the metal-oxygen bonding \(\mathrm{O}^{2-}\) related to oxides of Cu and Co) and oxygens on the surface. Finally, the analysis of XPS spectra related to ZCCO shows that it is composed of \({\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}\) , \({\mathrm{Co}}^{3+}\) , \({\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}\) , and \(0^{2-}\) that is in good agreement with the composition of \({\mathrm{CuCo}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}\) .
The FESEM and TEM images could be used for further investigating the morphology and structural properties of the as-prepared samples, verifying the formation of hollow structures. Fig. 1e shows the highly uniform spheres of CC-gly with the average diameter in the range of \(500{-}600\,\mathrm{nm}\) . Fig. 1f and \(\mathbf{g}\) show the formation of small CuCo-ZIF cubes on the exterior surface of CC-gly spheres. Directly calcinated \({\mathrm{CuCo}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}\) spheres from CC-gly precursor with small partially uniform grains on the surface are shown in Fig. 1h. As demonstrated in Fig. 1i and j, in the calcination step, the ZCC-gly spheres tend to accelerate the depletion of the interior structure and form hollow spheres.
With the purpose of confirming the formation of a hollow structure, the TEM images of ZCC-gly and ZCCO are demonstrated in Fig. 1k and l, respectively. The contrast difference of the dark edge and pale center displays the formation of the yolk-shell structure of ZCC-gly sample (Fig. 1k). Additionally, the TEM image of ZCCO reveals the formation of a double-shell hollow sphere.
The straightforward synthesis of the double-shell ZCCO hollow spheres is schematically demonstrated in Scheme 2. As shown, firstly the uniform CC-gly spheres were converted into the yolk-shell spheres utilizing a layer of bimetallic CuCo-ZIF. This phenomenon happens owing to the strong coordination of 2-methylimidazole with copper and cobalt ions of CC-gly sphere [46]. It tends to form a uniform shell of bimetallic CuCo-ZIF nanocrystals on the exterior surface of spheres. The formation of interior yolk-shell structure could be described by diffusion of
different cationic and anionic species along with the structure [32,47,48]. In this phenomenon, the smaller ions of solid structure tend to diffuse toward the surface that results in the growth of CuCo-ZIF shell over the whole solid sphere [49,50]. Therefore, a yolk-shell structure is formed by incorporated ions, which results in a well-defined interior void. Subsequently, the ZCC-gly based yolk-shell structure is converted to a double-shell \({\mathrm{CuCo}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}\) hollow sphere via a heat-treatment process.
## 3.2. Electrochemical evaluation
In order to evaluate the electrochemical performances of the designed structures as a supercapacitor electrode material, cyclic voltammetry (CV) and galvanostatic charge-discharge (GCD) tests were accompanied in a 3 M KOH as the electrolyte solution. Fig. 2a represents the CV of CCO and ZCCO electrodes at a constant scan rate \((20\,\mathrm{mV}\,\mathrm{s}^{-1})\) within an electrochemical window range of \(-0.1\) to \(0.55\,\mathrm{V}\) . The peaks of both samples revealed similar curves having a pair of redox peaks representing that the capacity is ascribed to the Faradaic redox reactions of \({\mathrm{Co}}^{4+}/{\mathrm{Co}}^{3+}\) and \(\mathrm{{Cu^{+}}/}\) \({\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}\) redox pairs [51,52]. It clearly shows the pseudo-capacitive features of these electrodes [53]. The electrochemical redox potentials of the cobalt and copper transitions are so close, which results in overlapped oxidation course [54]. Moreover, the comparison of current densities shows that ZCCO electrode has a significantly higher electrochemical capacity than that of CCO. Fig. 2b represents the characteristic CV plots of the ZCCO electrode at various scan rates of 5, 10, 20, and \(50\,\mathrm{mV}\,\mathrm{s}^{-1}\) . It can be observed that the current response is increased once the scan rate is increased, which represents the high rate capability of ZCCO electrode. This phenomenon shows the fast redox reactions of this electrode that is
the consequence of both increasing the anodic and reducing the cathodic peak current densities [55,56]. In addition, the stable shape of the peaks through increasing the scan rate suggests promoted mass transport and excellent electrochemical reversibility of ZCCO electrode. The redox peaks belong to the following reaction [57,58]:
\[
CuCO_2O_4+H_2O+e^-\leftrightarrow2CoOOH+CuOH
\]
\[
CoOOH+OH^-\leftrightarrow CoO_2+H_2O+e^-
\]
\[
CuOH+OH^{-}\leftrightarrow Cu(OH)_2+e^{-}
\]
The GCD analysis was utilized for further investigation of the energy storage properties of the fabricated electrodes. The GCD plots of both CCO and ZCCO electrodes at a constant current density of \(2\,\mathsf{A}\,\mathsf{g}^{-1}\) with a stable potential window of \(0.0{-}0.5\,\mathrm{V}\) are
demonstrated in Fig. 2c. Both electrodes show typical battery-type performance. This is not the same as electrostatic double-layer capacitors (EDLC) performance with the inverted "V" shape of galvanostatic charge-discharge plots [59]. Additionally, the discharge time of ZCCO electrode is longer than that of CCO electrode indicating higher capacity and good coulombic efficiency of it.
Up to now, the Faradaic electroactive structures are known and investigated as pseudocapacitive materials. More recently, electroactive materials (except Ru and Mn oxides) is expected to have battery-type nonlinear charge-discharge curves in alkaline electrolyte solutions. Thus, specific discharge capacity or specific capacity with \(C\,\mathsf{g}^{-1}\) unit is applied to investigate the energy storage performance of electrodes [60]. Accordingly, the specific capacity of CCO and ZCCO electrodes calculated at \(2\,\mathsf{A}\,\mathsf{g}^{-1}\) obtained from GCD curves are about 608 and \(701\,\mathsf{C}\,\mathsf{g}^{-1}\), respectively. In order to
check the energy storage performance of ZCCO electrode, GCD analysis was conducted utilizing current densities of \(2{-}20\,\mathsf{A}\,\mathsf{g}^{-1}\). The GCD curves of the ZCCO electrode revealed regular curves at different current densities that are in good agreement with attained CV results. Excellent electrochemical characteristics, high coulombic efficiency, and outstanding reversible redox properties of ZCCO electrodes during the charge/discharge process are confirmed according to symmetric GCD curves indicated in Fig. 2d. Additionally, it is noticeable that negligible iR drop even at high current densities could be related to high conductivity along with low internal resistance of the electrode, which are criterions of attaining high rate supercapacitors [61].
The rate capability of the GCD and ZCCO electrodes at various current densities are illustrated in Fig. 2e. Remarkably, the CCO electrode presented the specific capacity values of 608, 536, 484, 431, 361, and \(305\,\mathsf{C}\,\mathsf{g}^{-1}\), while the ZCCO electrode exhibits the specific capacity values of 701, 639, 581, 529, 475, and \(428\,\mathsf{C}\,\mathsf{g}^{-1}\) at the current densities of 2, 5, 10, 20, 40, and \(60\,\mathsf{A}\,\mathsf{g}^{-1}\), respectively. The decrease in capacity under high current densities is related to the fact that electrochemical reactions occur only on the surface molecules during the charge-discharge process [53,62]. In addition, it is noticeable that \(61\%\) of capacity retained at an ultrahigh current density of \(60\,\mathsf{A}\,\mathsf{g}^{-1}\), while it is reduced to \(50\%\) for the CCO electrode at the same condition.
The long-term life cycle is a key parameter for evaluating endurance stability of electrode materials. For this purpose, the electrode was examined for 6000 continuous charge-discharge cycles at a current density of \(10\,\mathsf{A}\,\mathsf{g}^{-1}\). As indicated in Fig. 2f, the ZCCO electrode maintained \(93.6\%\) of its initial capacity after 6000CD cycles indicating its excellent cycling stability. The high specific capacity and excellent cycle life of the ZCCO electrode is excitant in comparison with other previously reported metal oxide electrodes represented in Table S1.
## 3.3. Electrochemical performance of the ZCCO//rGO asymmetric device
The main shortcoming for the commercial development of supercapacitors is low energy density. It is important to improve the energy density of such devices along with preserving their high
power density [63]. The asymmetric configuration is a solution for improving the energy density of supercapacitors with using positive and negative electrodes that enables such devices to store more energy over a wide range of working potentials [64,65]. Generally, an EDLC material is used as anode and a Faradaic electrode as the cathode to enhance the energy density of the device due to spreading the operating cell voltage along with improving the specific capacity [66]. For this purpose, the ZCCO electrode with remarkable electrochemical performance is selected as a cathode and rGO electrode is applied as the negative power source electrode (anode). In a typical asymmetric test, the two as mentioned electrodes are fabricated with sandwich-like structure separated using a cellulose filter paper in a 3 M KOH solution as the electrolyte, sealed in a pouch-type polyethylene bag (Fig. 3a). As shown in Fig. 3b, the CV curves show the well-balanced charge between the rGO electrode as the EDLC anode electrode and the ZCCO electrode as the cathode battery-type electrode. The electrochemical experiments were conducted to verify the performance of the constructed HSC system. The CV curves of the ZCCO//rGO asymmetric device scanned with the rates in the range of \(10{-}50\,\mathrm{mV}\,\mathrm{s}^{-1}\) is demonstrated in Fig. 3c. A very small shift is presented by changing scan rates and the device maintained perfectly its CV shape for all the scan rates indicating superior rate capability and reversibility of it. It can be also found that the working voltage of the fabricated ZCCO//rGO asymmetric cell can be extended up to \(1.6\,\mathrm{V}\) with reduction-oxidation peaks and without the effect of oxygen/hydrogen evolution. According to the second order proportion of energy density with the potential window, the superior energy density of such HSCs seems to be achievable, which is much higher than that of conventional activated carbon symmetric capacitors (0.80– \(1.00\,\mathrm{V}\)). Fig. 3d demonstrates the GCD curves of the ZCCO//rGO HSC at different current densities \((1{-}20\,\mathsf{A}\,\mathsf{g}^{-1})\) using a voltage window of 0 to \(1.6\,\mathrm{V}\). Nearly symmetric charge-discharge time response and low iR drop not only confirm the excellent rate capability and low internal resistance of the device but also verify its high columbic efficiency.
The rate capability of the device based on its specific capacity at different current densities is presented in Fig. 3e. The specific capacities of 104, 126, 141, 158 and \(173\,\mathsf{C}\,\mathsf{g}^{-1}\) were attained by
varying current densities in the range of \(0.5{-}20\,\mathsf{A}\,\mathsf{g}^{-1}\). The excellent rate capability could be concluded according to the retention of \(60\%\) capacity via varying current density. In addition, the cyclic capability of the device revealed \(5.9\%\) decline in specific capacity after 5000 cycles at the current density of \(10\,\mathsf{A}\,\mathsf{g}^{-1}\), representing the superior stability of the device (Fig. 3f). The power and energy density of HSC devices are two important parameters for the recognition of the operational efficiency of energy storage devices. In fact, the Ragone plot is applied for the comparison of different types of energy storage devices [67]. As indicated in Fig. 3g, the assembled HSC revealed the maximum energy density of \(38.4\,\mathsf{W}\,\mathsf{h}\,\mathsf{k}\mathsf{g}^{-1}\) at a power density equal to \(800\,\mathsf{W}\,\mathsf{k}\mathsf{g}^{-1}\). It shows that the achieved energy density and power density values of our device are comparable with or higher than previously reported energy storage devices. \(\mathsf{C u C o}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4}/\mathsf{C u O}//\mathsf{A C}\) \((18\,\mathsf{W h}\,\mathsf{k g}^{-1})\) [68], \(\mathsf{C u C o}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4}//\mathsf{A C}\) \((25.2\,\mathsf{W h}\,\mathsf{k g}^{-1})\) [69], \(\mathsf{C u C o}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{C o}(\mathsf{O H})_{2}//\mathsf{A G}\) [70], \((19.2\,\mathsf{W h}\,\mathsf{k g}^{-1})\), CCO-NS//HCP-CNF [54] \((25.1\,\mathsf{W h}\,\mathsf{k g}^{-1})\), \(\mathsf{C u C o}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{N i}_{0.5}\mathsf{C o}_{0.5}(\mathsf{O H})_{2}//\mathsf{A C}\) [57] \((32\,\mathsf{W h}\,\mathsf{k g}^{-1})\), \(\mathsf{C u C o O-H}//\mathsf{A C}\) [71] \((32.2\,\mathsf{W h}\,\mathsf{k g}^{-1})\), \(\mathsf{C u C o}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4}/\mathsf{C u C o}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4}\) [72] \((16.9\,\mathsf{W h}\,\mathsf{k g}^{-1})\), \(\mathsf{C u C o}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{C u C o}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4}//\mathsf{A C}\) [73] \((18\,\mathsf{W h}\,\mathsf{k g}^{-1})\), and \(\mathsf{C u C o}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4}/\mathsf{C u O}//\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}\) [74] \((33\,\mathsf{W h}\,\mathsf{k g}^{-1})\).
The investigation of the above results shows that this HSC could be applied for different feasible applications in hybrid energy storage devices. The excellent efficiency of ZCCO electrode represented here could be due to different reasons such as double-shell hollow microsphere morphology, which reduces the diffusion/transfer distance of ions/electrons along with higher cycling stability. In addition, the proposed spinel \(\left(\mathsf{C u C o}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4}\right)\) have both higher redox reaction with greater active sites and conductivity than single metal oxides. Finally, the high specific surface area can improve the accessibility of electroactive sites for the electrolyte to perform electrochemical reactions. Accordingly, the ZCCO electrode is a promising candidate for the development of high-performance HSC devices for energy storage applications. With the purpose of proving the real application of the ZCCO//rGO device, an applied test is implemented finally. As shown in Fig. 3h, two devices connected in series can successfully power green, blue and red round LEDs, respectively, for more than \(20\,\mathrm{min}\).
## 4. Conclusions
In summary, we have successfully developed a MOF-assisted method for the production of double-shell \(\mathsf{C u C o}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4}\) hollow microspheres. It includes a simultaneously dissolving and transferring of metal ions into bimetallic \(\mathsf{C u C o-Z I F}\) MOF via a cation-exchange reaction followed by the calcination of final structure in air atmosphere. The ZCC-gly treated sample forms a yolk-shell structure while the annealing exchanges the sample into a double-shell hollow sphere. According to the unique structure of synthesized hollow sphere and good accessibility of inner active sites, the results obtained from the systems with three- and two-electrodes revealed that the ZCCO hollow microsphere electrode is a promising candidate for high-performance supercapacitor. The high specific capacity of \(701\mathsf{C}\,\mathsf{g}^{-1}\) at \(2\,\mathsf{A}\,\mathsf{g}^{-1}\) and an excellent cycling efficiency of \(93.6\%\) remaining after 6000 GCD successive cycles have been achieved for this electrode. Furthermore, the outstanding performance of as-fabricated asymmetric supercapacitor was provided with maximum power and energy densities of \(16\,\mathsf{W}\,\mathsf{k g}^{-1}\) and \(38.4\,\mathsf{W}\,\mathsf{h}\,\mathsf{k g}^{-1}\), respectively, that is greater than that of conventional supercapacitors. On the other hand, the proposed structure could be a step of progress towards the construction of hybrid electrodes and materials for different applications like catalysis and electrocatalysis, fuel cells, lithium-ion batteries, and gas/ biosensors.
